CAT = "excptDispense"; SEG = "DSP"
# Domain 4B (13 of 100): tasks 4B.1 to 4B.12, knowledge K.92 to K.107.
Q = [
("Which feature can suggest that a prescription is forged?",
 "Directions spelled out in full", ["Prescriber's usual shorthand", "Today's date in the date line", "A signature in blue ink"],
 "DEA's Pharmacist's Guide to Prescription Fraud lists directions written in full with no abbreviations, very neat handwriting and mixed ink colors as warning signs."),
("A prescription's quantity looks changed from 10 to 40 in a different ink. What does the technician do?",
 "Hold it and show the pharmacist", ["Fill the original quantity of 10", "Fill 40 and note the concern", "Ask the patient which is right"],
 "An altered prescription is not filled on the technician's own judgment. The pharmacist verifies it with the prescriber and decides what to do."),
("In the NDC 0378-1805-01, what does the first segment identify?",
 "The labeler", ["The package size", "The drug strength", "The lot number"],
 "21 CFR 207.33: the first NDC segment is the FDA-assigned labeler code. The second is the product code and the third the package code."),
("Two bottles of the same drug and strength have NDCs that differ only in the last segment. What differs?",
 "The package size or type", ["The manufacturer or labeler", "The active ingredient", "The dosage form"],
 "The third NDC segment is the package code, which identifies package size and type. The same product in 100-count and 500-count bottles differs there."),
("Under FDA's final rule, what NDC format becomes uniform on March 7, 2033?",
 "A 12-digit NDC", ["A 10-digit NDC", "An 11-digit NDC", "A 14-digit NDC"],
 "FDA's final rule revising the NDC format and barcode requirements adopts a uniform 12-digit NDC, taking effect March 7, 2033."),
("Why does the technician note the lot number of the stock bottle used to fill a prescription?",
 "So a recall can be traced", ["So the patient can reorder it", "So the claim pays faster", "So the expiration can be extended"],
 "Recalls name specific lots. Recording the lot used lets the pharmacy find which patients received an affected product."),
("A pharmacy stocks an automated dispensing system at a nursing home. When are the drugs inside counted as dispensed?",
 "When the system releases a dose", ["When the pharmacy loads the machine", "At the end of each month", "When the invoice is paid"],
 "DEA treats drugs in an automated dispensing system as pharmacy stock until the system dispenses them on a single-dose basis under a valid prescription."),
("Before reconstituting an antibiotic powder, what does the technician confirm on the label?",
 "The diluent type and volume", ["The patient's insurance tier", "The prescriber's NPI", "The bottle's NDC length"],
 "The manufacturer's label states which diluent to use and how much. The wrong liquid or amount gives the wrong concentration."),
("Which item must appear on the label of a dispensed controlled substance prescription?",
 "The prescription serial number", ["The patient's diagnosis and ICD code", "The prescriber's DEA number", "The pharmacist's home address"],
 "21 CFR 1306.14 and 1306.24: the label shows the date filled, pharmacy name and address, serial number, patient, prescriber, directions and any cautionary statements."),
("Which auxiliary label fits a prescription for hydroxyzine?",
 "May cause drowsiness", ["Take on an empty stomach", "Avoid sunlight exposure", "Refrigerate, do not freeze"],
 "Hydroxyzine is an antihistamine that can make patients drowsy, so they are warned about driving and machinery."),
("Which auxiliary label belongs on clotrimazole cream?",
 "For external use only", ["Shake well before using", "Take with a full glass of water", "Do not chew or crush"],
 "Topical clotrimazole is applied to the skin for fungal infections such as athlete's foot, so the label warns against swallowing it."),
("Which auxiliary label belongs on an extended-release tablet?",
 "Swallow whole; do not crush", ["Dissolve the tablet under the tongue", "Shake well before using", "For the eye only"],
 "Crushing or chewing an extended-release tablet can release the whole dose at once."),
("A drug requires a Medication Guide. When is it given to the patient?",
 "Each time the drug is dispensed", ["Only with the first fill", "Only if the patient asks", "Only for children's prescriptions"],
 "FDA says a Medication Guide is dispensed to the patient in paper form when the product is dispensed, so it goes with fills, not just the first one."),
("For which products must a patient package insert be dispensed with every prescription?",
 "Oral contraceptives and estrogens", ["All antibiotics", "All Schedule II controlled substances", "Insulin pens and rescue inhalers"],
 "FDA requires patient package inserts for oral contraceptives (21 CFR 310.501) and estrogens (21 CFR 310.515). Other PPIs are voluntary."),
("What does an FDA-approved Instructions for Use (IFU) give the patient?",
 "Step-by-step use directions", ["The drug's price history", "The prescriber's contact details", "The plan's formulary tier"],
 "An IFU gives detailed steps for products with complicated use, such as pens and inhalers, including preparation, administration, storage and disposal."),
("What is a Risk Evaluation and Mitigation Strategy (REMS)?",
 "An FDA drug safety program", ["A plan to lower drug prices", "A wholesaler's return policy", "A state refill limit"],
 "FDA can require a REMS for certain drugs with serious safety concerns, to make sure their benefits outweigh their risks. Only a few drugs need one."),
("A pharmacy mails a controlled substance prescription. How must the outer package look?",
 "Plain, with no sign of contents", ["Labeled with the drug's schedule", "Marked 'Controlled Substance Inside'", "Stamped with the pharmacy's DEA number"],
 "Postal guidance cited in DEA's Pharmacist's Manual: the inner container is labeled and sealed, and the outer wrapper is plain with nothing showing what is inside."),
("Under Medicaid counseling standards, how must a mail-order pharmacy offer counseling?",
 "Through a toll-free phone line", ["In person at the patient's home", "By printed leaflet only", "Only if the patient calls first"],
 "42 CFR 456.705 requires mail-order pharmacies to provide toll-free telephone service for long-distance calls so patients can be counseled."),
("The pharmacist recommends plain guaifenesin for a patient's chest congestion. Which product does the technician hand over?",
 "A single-ingredient expectorant", ["Guaifenesin with pseudoephedrine", "Dextromethorphan with no guaifenesin", "A nighttime product with an antihistamine"],
 "The Drug Facts panel lists active ingredients. Matching the pharmacist's recommendation means a single-ingredient guaifenesin product, an expectorant."),
("On an OTC Drug Facts label, which section lists the ingredient and amount in each dose?",
 "Active ingredient", ["Inactive ingredients", "Other information", "Questions or comments"],
 "21 CFR 201.66 places Active ingredient, with the amount per dosage unit, first under the Drug Facts title, followed by Purpose."),
("Which drug is handled as a hazardous drug, with chemotherapy gloves?",
 "Methotrexate", ["Amoxicillin", "Lisinopril", "Levothyroxine"],
 "OSHA's hazardous drug guidance names methotrexate, cyclophosphamide and fluorouracil among drugs that contaminate surfaces and protective equipment during handling."),
("Under DEA's definition of an ultimate user, who besides the patient may receive the patient's controlled substance prescription?",
 "Someone in the patient's household", ["Any adult who knows the patient", "The patient's employer, with a letter", "A neighbor with a signed note"],
 "21 U.S.C. 802(27) defines an ultimate user as the patient or a member of the patient's household. DEA's Pharmacist's Manual says dispensing to anyone else is distributing."),
("A retail pharmacy sends a controlled substance prescription to its central fill pharmacy. What does it write on the original?",
 "CENTRAL FILL", ["TRANSFERRED OUT", "VOID", "PARTIAL"],
 "21 CFR 1306.15 and 1306.27: the retail pharmacy writes CENTRAL FILL on the face of the original and records the central fill pharmacy's details."),
("When measuring a liquid in a graduated cylinder, where is the volume read?",
 "At the bottom of the meniscus", ["At the top of the meniscus, from above", "At the cylinder's top line", "Wherever the liquid touches the glass"],
 "Water-based liquids curve down in the middle. Reading the bottom of that curve at eye level gives an accurate volume."),
]
